('Situated','Angela Duckworth','when my English teacher assigned Virginia Woolf’s A Room of One’s Own. A good idea, Woolf said, is like a fish. It catches on the line of your thought and, if you’re lucky and have patience, you stay with it as it darts and sinks and flashes “hither and thither” in your mind. But what often happens before you can reel in this idea of yours is an interruption. You’re asked to make breakfast or dinner or to answer the doorbell—or, in my mother’s case, to scratch my father’s back (literally). When you return, the fish is gone. And no matter how hard you try to find it, it’s now hiding and does not return.','287'),
